== 16. The Times in Which We Live Call for Secret Prayer

Consider that the times in which we live call aloud for secret prayer. Hell seems to have broken loose, and men to have turned into devils incarnate. Land-destroying and soul-damning wickednesses walk up and down the streets with a harlot’s forehead, without the least check or control. “Yet you have a harlot’s forehead; you refuse to be ashamed… Are they ashamed of the abomination they have committed? No, they have no shame at all; they do not even know how to blush” (Jer 3:3; 6:15). They had sinned away shame instead of being ashamed of sin. Custom in sin had quite banished all sense of sin and all shame for sin, so that they would not allow nature to draw her veil of blushing before their great abominations. They were like Caligula, a wicked emperor, who used to say of himself that there was nothing in himself he loved better than that he could not be ashamed. The same words are repeated in Jeremiah 8:12. I will leave the prudent reader to judge how applicable these scriptures are to the present time.

But what does the prophet do when they were as bold in sin and as shameless as so many harlots? You#super[+] may see that in Jeremiah 13:17: “But if you#super[+] do not listen, my soul will weep in secret places,” or secrecies, “because of your#super[+] pride; my eyes will weep bitterly and overflow with tears.” The Hebrew is “weeping weep,” or “shedding tears shed tears”; the doubling of the verb shows the bitter and grievous lamentation he would make for them. Now they had grown to such a height of sin and wickedness that they were above all shame and blushing; now they had grown so proud, so hardened, so obstinate, so rebellious, so mad on wickedness, that no mercies could melt them or win them, and no threatenings or judgments could in any way terrify them or stop them. The prophet goes into a corner; he withdraws into the most secret places, and there he weeps bitterly; there he weeps as if he were resolved to drown himself in his own tears. When the springs of sorrow rise high, a Christian turns his back on company and withdraws into places of the greatest privacy, so that he may vent his sorrow and grief before the Lord more freely and more fully.

Ah, England, England! What pride, luxury, lasciviousness, licentiousness, wantonness, drunkenness, cruelties, injustice, oppressions, fornications, adulteries, falsehoods, hypocrisy, bribery, atheism, horrid blasphemies, and hellish impieties are now to be found rampant in your midst! Ah, England! England! How the Lord’s Sabbaths are profaned, pure ordinances despised, Scriptures rejected, the Spirit resisted and derided, the righteous reviled, wickedness favored, and Christ crucified afresh many thousand times a day by these cursed practices! Ah, England! England! If our forefathers were alive, how sadly they would blush to see such a horribly degenerate posterity as is to be found in your midst! How our forefathers’ hospitality is turned into riot and luxury, their frugality into pride and extravagance, their simplicity into cunning, their sincerity into hypocrisy, their charity into cruelty, their chastity into sexual immorality and wantonness, their sobriety into drunkenness, their plain dealing into dissembling, their works of compassion into works of oppression, and their love for the people of God into utter enmity against the people of God! And what is the voice of all these crying abominations, but: every Christian to his closet, every Christian to his closet, and there weep bitterly with weeping Jeremiah for all these great abominations by which God is openly dishonored. Oh, weep in secret for the sins of those who openly glory in their sins, which should be their greatest shame. Oh, blush in secret for those who are past all blushing for their sins; for who knows but that the whole land may fare the better for the sake of a few who are mourners in secret? But however it goes with the nation, those who mourn in secret for the abominations of the times may be confident that when sweeping judgments come upon the land, the Lord will hide them in the secret chambers of His providence. He will set a secret mark of deliverance on the foreheads of those who mourn in secret for the crying sins of the present day, as He did on theirs in Ezekiel 9:4–6.
